      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-225687",
      ":question/problem": "Let $X_{1}, X_{2}, \u2026, X_{25}$ be a random sample of size $n = 25$ conducted without replacement from a population of $N = 100$ individuals where $35%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. If $\u03a6(z)$ denotes the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution, then the probability that the proportion of individuals in the sample having the characteristic is greater than $0.4$ is approximately equal to $1 - \u03a6($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."
